            <ArticleTitle>Kawasaki disease associated with reactive hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1116-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/INF.0b013e31817ecb6d</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a case of Kawasaki disease with significant coronary artery aneurysms subsequently associated with reactive hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis in a young child with low T-cell perforin expression and NK-cell dysfunction. The patient was treated with a selective T-cell costimulation modulator in an effort to regulate T-cells. This case is unique for several reasons: (1) the severe degree of coronary artery aneurysms; (2) low T-cell perforin and NK-cell values; and (3) treatment with a selective T-cell costimulation modulator, none of which has been described in prior cases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
